{{block center|<math>\sin A \cos \tfrac{1}{2}a \cos \tfrac{1}{2}d \operatorname{cosec} \tfrac{1}{2}y = \sin C \cos \tfrac{1}{2}b \cos \tfrac{1}{2}C \operatorname{cosec} \tfrac{1}{2}y</math>;}}
{{block center|whence <math>\frac{\sin A}{\cos \tfrac{1}{2}b \cos \tfrac{1}{2}c} = \frac{\sin C}{\cos \tfrac{1}{2}a \cos \tfrac{1}{2}d}</math>.}}
This is the proposition corresponding to the relation <math>A+C=\pi</math> for a plane quadrilateral. Also we obtain in a similar manner the theorem
{{block center|<math>\frac{\sin \tfrac{1}{2}x}{\sin B \cos \tfrac{1}{2}b} = \frac{\sin \tfrac{1}{2}y}{\sin A \cos \tfrac{1}{2}d}</math>,}}
analogous to the theorem for a plane quadrilateral, that the diagonals are proportional to the sines of the angles opposite to them. Also the chords <math>AB</math>, <math>BC</math>, <math>CD</math>, <math>DA</math> are equal to <math>2 \sin \tfrac{1}{2}a</math>, <math>2 \sin \tfrac{1}{2}b</math>, <math>2 \sin \tfrac{1}{2}c</math>, <math>2 \sin \tfrac{1}{2}d</math> respectively, and the plane quadrilateral formed by these chords is inscribed in the same circle as the spherical quadrilateral; hence by Ptolemy's theorem for a plane quadrilateral we obtain the analogous theorem for a spherical one
